# Line endings, declared rather than left to the client.
#
# **Git for Windows sets `core.autocrlf=true` by default**, which converts line
# endings *on the way out of the repository* — shell scripts included. A
# `bin/doctor.sh` checked out that way fails under Git Bash and WSL with:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# **That is the least legible error of the family**: it names an interpreter
# that exists, with an invisible character on the end. And `README.md` sends a
# Windows user to `bin/doctor.sh` first, so **the first gesture the project
# asks for is the one that breaks**.
#
# The files in this repository are LF; the problem is not here, it is at the
# boundary — which is why it is invisible to anyone developing on macOS or
# Linux and systematic for anyone cloning on Windows with the defaults.
#
# **This changes nothing for an existing clone.** Somebody already installed
# needs `git add --renormalize .` or a fresh clone.
#
# Reported as a missing guard against a known behaviour, not as an observed
# failure — no Windows machine was available to reproduce it. Issue #107.
#
# **What has since been checked, and what still has not.** `git check-attr`
# confirms the rule reaches every `bin/*.sh` and both LaTeX templates, and
# `git ls-files --eol` reports **`i/lf` for all 228 tracked files** — so
# nothing was already stored with a carriage return, which the rule alone
# would not have undone. `tests/test_core.py::LineEndingsAreDeclared` pins all
# three, and goes red against a tree that has a `i/crlf` blob in its index.
#
# **None of that is a Windows test.** It says the declaration is present,
# applies, and has nothing to repair — not that a Windows clone now works.
# That still needs somebody on Windows running `bin/doctor.sh`.

# Let git decide what is text, and normalise it to LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executables and anything an interpreter reads by shebang: never CRLF.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.tex   text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never rewrites a byte of it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.ico   binary
